 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, you should stay indoors as much as possible.** \n\nThe air quality in Kuala Lumpur is rated as **Unhealthy**, with Air Quality Index (AQI) readings across the city ranging between **155 and 192**. \n\n### Why This Matters for Asthma:\n* **Primary Pollutant:** The main pollutant driving the high AQI is **PM2.5** (fine particulate matter). These microscopic particles can easily bypass your nose and throat, penetrating deep into your lungs and triggering airway inflammation, wheezing, and asthma attacks.\n* **Risk Level:** At an AQI above 150, everyone may begin to experience adverse health effects, while sensitive groups—especially people with asthma or other respiratory conditions—are at risk of experiencing more serious, immediate symptoms.\n\n### Recommended Precautions:\n1. **Avoid Outdoor Exertion:** Stay indoors and skip any outdoor exercise or prolonged walking.\n2. **Keep Outdoor Air Out:** Close your windows and doors. If you use air conditioning, set it to **recirculate** mode so you aren't pulling smoky/polluted air in from outside. Run a HEPA air purifier if you have one.\n3. **Mask Up if You Must Go Out:** If going outside is unavoidable, wear a well-fitted **N95 or KN95** mask. Standard cloth or surgical masks do not effectively filter out fine PM2.5 particles.\n4. **Keep Medication Handy:** Keep your rescue inhaler (e.g., Ventolin/salbutamol) within reach at all times, and closely follow your asthma action plan if you feel your chest tightening or start coughing.",
